#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "8ebce398-b228-51b0-8506-e0c6124478a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6852" :question/problem "Find the differential for $f(x,y) = x^2 \\sin y.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124293 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/id #uuid "bb3ab0dc-7e26-58f5-a0c2-564c01c77f2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6854" :question/problem "Given a cone, find the differential $\\textrm d V$ for the volume $V$ of the cone if the radius is $r=6$ and the height is $h = 3.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the cone is $V = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi r^2h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=6$ and $h=3,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(6,3) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 6\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{1}{3}\\pi \\cdot {6}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+12\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0c4816ed-fba0-5dc7-94ba-646b875d653d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6875" :question/problem "If $f (x, y) = xe^y ,$ use the differential $\\textrm d f$ to estimate the change $\\Delta f$ in the value of $f(x, y)$ from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(5,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(5,0) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+5{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+5\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(5 + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx 1 \\, \\Delta x + 5 \\, \\Delta y.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(5,0)$ to $(2,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-5=-3, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+5\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "febc72d4-b1b8-52c9-a805-013b30695077" :question/math-academy-id "e-6876" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)$ using a differential of the function $f(x,y) = \\sin x + \\cos y$ at the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $\\left( \\pi, 0 \\right),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,\\text{d}x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\pi + \\Delta x, 0 + \\Delta y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\Delta f \\approx  -1  \\,  \\Delta x + 0  \\,  \\Delta y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sin (3) + \\cos (0.2)= f(3,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(3,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(\\pi, 0)$ to $(3,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =3-\\pi ≈-0.141, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈-1(-0.141)+0(0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.141.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,0.2) & ≈f(\\pi ,0)+0.141 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+0.141 \\\\  & ≈0+1+0.141 \\\\  & ≈1.141.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1622e7f6-1a2d-5346-9162-2c7d1473e6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338400} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b817de-88ae-5ad0-92ad-2db6100597db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338401} {:answer/id #uuid "39e40d4c-69e8-5154-be4b-00e6112a28c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338402} {:answer/id #uuid "61409cad-8b9f-597f-b024-4f18482d10ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338403} {:answer/id #uuid "95266bcb-e5cd-5656-a939-75a0967147f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61409cad-8b9f-597f-b024-4f18482d10ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338403} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15be319-2b80-55b8-95ab-a65f4b03e863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee1f88d-3f94-5266-a2de-5d2046b403ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-167166" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{2y-z},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}x+2x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}y-x{e}^{2y-z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(-1,2,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(-1,2,3) & ={e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}y-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2(2)-3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =e\\,\\text{d}x-2e\\,\\text{d}y+e\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(-1+Δx,2+Δy,3+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(-1,2,3)$ to $(1,0,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-(-1)=2, \\\\ Δy & =0-2=-2 \\\\ Δz & =1-3=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈e\\,Δx-2e\\,Δy+e\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈e\\cdot (2)-2e\\cdot (-2)+e\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ≈4e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $4e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156375a1-0669-562c-a408-c68017992149" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338407} {:answer/id #uuid "b62aa180-1842-5f79-8c9c-8c5b96ebb575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338408} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2369fe-b318-5409-be82-df859c61de9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338409} {:answer/id #uuid "4e766824-14a1-54ae-83db-edb1298a01bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338410} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f86c21-683d-512c-ba8d-273c5a8702b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2369fe-b318-5409-be82-df859c61de9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338409} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b71b3b-fc7a-58ab-a94a-6a95f0453e8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd59d308-6e41-5fd0-8b0f-1ea88730848b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167168" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y+\\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,1,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,1,1) & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+1\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,1+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈Δx+Δy+Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,1,1)$ to $(2,3,4),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2-1=1, \\\\ Δy & =3-1=2 \\\\ Δz & =4-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈Δx+Δy+Δz \\\\  & ≈1+2+3 \\\\  & ≈6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d359091b-d471-567a-83c2-07c7d3c45991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338414} {:answer/id #uuid "c03f28f0-525d-5df5-9356-5befa19004c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338415} {:answer/id #uuid "5149d8f4-b99e-5867-88cd-a23d8632b5f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338416} {:answer/id #uuid "06a01ca9-3dc0-55f9-8a85-7a220c7c8be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338417} {:answer/id #uuid "93f81494-6c98-5aeb-86db-dfe93ec432ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d359091b-d471-567a-83c2-07c7d3c45991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338414} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7f1e05-fc03-519c-9816-58a27106844d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da6bb9d5-5b64-5e69-823c-947a664f9346" :question/math-academy-id "q-167170" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1}=f(0.2,0.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.2,0.1).$\n\nMoving from $(0,0)$ to $(0.2,0.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.2-0=0.2, \\\\ Δy & =0.1-0=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)+\\frac{1}{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.05 \\\\  & =0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.15.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.2,0.1) & ≈f(0,0)+0.15 \\\\ \\sqrt{{e}^{0.2}+0.1} & ≈\\sqrt{{e}^{0}+0}+0.15 \\\\  & =1+0.15 \\\\  & =1.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad66c4fa-3e3f-5ca7-87c6-d4664253161c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338421} {:answer/id #uuid "16cadda8-e28e-5db4-8ef7-e6850c66d716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.482" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338422} {:answer/id #uuid "e267b74c-b376-55c6-8f03-ac16d0bfa2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.652" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338423} {:answer/id #uuid "636f7633-040f-53b2-9ce8-f99518ce5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338424} {:answer/id #uuid "7b524090-0612-5634-bad0-3ee845f687ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.274" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636f7633-040f-53b2-9ce8-f99518ce5881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.359" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338424} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4f1d83-1e8d-5aa6-8ba2-7b3962fc1a49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "240dd5e9-7623-5d41-a562-b45731698fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-167171" :question/problem "Estimate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)$ at the point $({\\pi }^{2},0).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,Δx-\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & ≈f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & ≈\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0179cc13-dd1b-5d3b-9518-143e2445e69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122825} {:answer/id #uuid "35d534f9-0842-5c39-b538-5a116c35e692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.556" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122826} {:answer/id #uuid "50ae4762-cb31-5fe1-b0bf-2a2dadbc049b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.873" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122827} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5b9594-1bd0-5656-9186-2c9c0bd4c3d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122828} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff33b79-6cc3-577d-9c7d-6711a937ed8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0179cc13-dd1b-5d3b-9518-143e2445e69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.417" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122825} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf3930e-171a-5983-a3fc-5f746e20f57e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4f3d8c4-d372-512f-a12b-325fd0cb0744" :question/math-academy-id "q-37281" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}+\\sqrt{y}$ at the point $(8,144).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(8,144),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {8}^{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{144}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(8+Δx,144+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{12}\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{24}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150}=f(10,150),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(10,150).$\n\nMoving from $(8,144)$ to $(10,150),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-8=2, \\\\ Δy & =150-144=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{12}(2)+\\frac{1}{24}(6) \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & ≈0.417.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.417.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,150) & ≈f(8,144)+0.417 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{10}+\\sqrt{150} & ≈\\sqrt[3]{8}+\\sqrt{144}+0.417 \\\\  & ≈2+12+0.417 \\\\  & ≈14.417.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8067b52c-da04-51be-83e7-1edb3b741faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}dr + \\frac{1}{8}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122837} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cfa7a3-067d-5243-9361-9adabafe2403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}dr + 8dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122838} {:answer/id #uuid "300d0677-dab4-5c46-b293-044cf57f1aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}dr + 4dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122839} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf20e9e-9e91-5e6b-a6ce-009dc2c92f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "πdr + 4dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122840} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdb5e3d-2da4-548e-92b9-49e537184e4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{8}dr + \\frac{1}{8}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3cfa7a3-067d-5243-9361-9adabafe2403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}dr + 8dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122838} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bb73f1-2c84-58a3-bb35-e53f79a07f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9652424-c2ee-5706-a3c2-84b62902c6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37295" :question/problem "Given a sector of a circle, find the differential $\\text{d}S$ for the surface area $S$ if the radius is $r=4$ and the central angle is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the sector is $S=\\frac{\\theta }{2}{r}^{2}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(r,\\theta ) & =(\\frac{∂S}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂S}{∂\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =r\\theta \\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(4,\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}r+8\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff4e387-63e7-5f7b-87b1-c005d396c662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xdx}{x + y^{2}} + \\frac{2ydy}{x + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122855} {:answer/id #uuid "c301c3e7-1645-5ecf-8752-8fafbfe877f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dx}{x + y^{2}} + \\frac{2ydy}{x + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122856} {:answer/id #uuid "c9528693-d3a6-537a-9a1a-8be6ce9e29aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2dx}{x + y^{2}} - \\frac{2dy}{x + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122857} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ebb781-d343-5f08-9b34-71ead3dc5b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + y^{2})dx + (x + y^{2})dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122858} {:answer/id #uuid "0c08229d-6c25-55f1-ae56-5b1e62eeac54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dx}{x + y^{2}} + \\frac{ydy}{x + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c301c3e7-1645-5ecf-8752-8fafbfe877f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dx}{x + y^{2}} + \\frac{2ydy}{x + y^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122856} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7d02a2-0bf4-5dec-b00c-6b2655731205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d0cda7b-1864-5320-acd1-fb1c921ad388" :question/math-academy-id "q-37307" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x+{y}^{2}}+\\frac{2y\\,\\text{d}y}{x+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85e0b9d0-f53d-502a-9d65-62acd6ab24a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16πdr + 4πdh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122861} {:answer/id #uuid "b79aa541-fdd4-5caf-a922-ad52df4ddcfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8πdr + 2πdh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122862} {:answer/id #uuid "d037942b-7503-53cd-adf0-bf2686a9d16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16dr + 4dh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122863} {:answer/id #uuid "0245b143-616f-5526-8a1c-96011e1acda0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4πdr + 16πdh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122864} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3cb6bd-d472-5e9b-b277-996d33e55d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4πdr + πdh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e0b9d0-f53d-502a-9d65-62acd6ab24a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16πdr + 4πdh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122861} :answer-field/id #uuid "85596a76-8dca-580a-b0aa-54ebac420949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35be243b-50e1-5aab-aebe-0bcb17e84ebe" :question/math-academy-id "q-37308" :question/problem "Given a cylinder, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the cylinder if the radius is $r=2$ and the height is $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the cylinder is $V=\\pi {r}^{2}h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =2\\pi rh\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi {r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=2$ and $h=4,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,4) & =2\\pi \\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}r+\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =16\\pi \\,\\text{d}r+4\\pi \\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f7d7b48-d777-5bde-a527-4096206df7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}x+(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338428} {:answer/id #uuid "2e210cd7-3f99-5169-88f7-0dbc6f4eb71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338429} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6e71fe-a320-54fb-8bf9-a4c2dd064d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338430} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c3a8be-af34-5c06-844e-80ebeed48651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338431} {:answer/id #uuid "c8904ec3-049b-5e77-b477-9b4a5375ee4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2})\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d6e71fe-a320-54fb-8bf9-a4c2dd064d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338430} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57af259-1b0e-5aa1-b2e3-3cb083ce5a75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b322579f-6a5d-5a60-9e95-cf23bfdd0f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79739" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{x}^{3}y-{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(2x+3{x}^{2}y)\\text{d}x+({x}^{3}-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "720aabbe-d44f-58f3-a140-43ba4308ef22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338435} {:answer/id #uuid "94326c99-0d2a-5aa6-902f-278698795c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\text{d}x+x\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338436} {:answer/id #uuid "5e596b34-f58d-5c80-82ba-b5d2eb7330ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\text{d}x+{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338437} {:answer/id #uuid "499c488d-133e-5f60-9315-2c81942a3293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338438} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1aeb81-e6f6-5889-9f77-be256ba98d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "499c488d-133e-5f60-9315-2c81942a3293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338438} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd3a7fc-b7d1-5715-93d6-7ac86471fe9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac95f161-582a-578b-861c-0c210b1f8926" :question/math-academy-id "q-79741" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81994aaf-c309-5495-b0a8-c52a22e8e5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x\\,\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338442} {:answer/id #uuid "1227fabd-1c36-5a43-9eee-2c3290995ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338443} {:answer/id #uuid "2c120c4c-372a-5809-b259-a333478c8dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x}+\\text{d}y+\\frac{3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z}{3{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338444} {:answer/id #uuid "11c3ee75-5857-5ad4-b8a1-e9bc647e20bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x\\,\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{2y\\text{d}y}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{2z\\,\\text{d}z}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338445} {:answer/id #uuid "58abffe3-350a-5e77-b264-3a865c40fa44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{y}+\\frac{3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z}{3{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81994aaf-c309-5495-b0a8-c52a22e8e5a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x\\,\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338442} :answer-field/id #uuid "05134ab9-7df7-53d4-81be-f564ab42cfb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5e954b3-7b19-5111-a9b1-97da95bb6c84" :question/math-academy-id "q-79742" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\,\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f9271d-eae0-56e7-9ee6-5c5504d56e35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338449} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7973b-8d49-51e6-a99f-10b66315aff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338450} {:answer/id #uuid "8c03b9b3-8c0a-599c-ac7a-98c129752ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{d}a+10\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338451} {:answer/id #uuid "4b735f67-8cba-5668-8676-0ea01106840e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30\\,\\text{d}a+15\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338452} {:answer/id #uuid "980da7f4-cc2b-580b-8c32-040f308090ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{d}a+15\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d7973b-8d49-51e6-a99f-10b66315aff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338450} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd52fd15-55cf-59b6-a257-dcb70f56f994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "385e16b4-f8da-593a-b039-318c70552c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79745" :question/problem "Given a rectangular pyramid, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular pyramid if the base is a square with the side of $a=6$ and the height is $h=5.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the rectangular pyramid is $V=\\frac{1}{3}{a}^{2}h.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,h) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{2ah}{3}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=6$ and $h=5,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(6,5) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 6\\cdot 5}{3}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{{6}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5cac7a4-46af-555c-9c0c-2c2e740a76b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+5\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338456} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed600a-5be6-506b-b39e-adaa571f4ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338457} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a9cbb7-dd32-556b-9a6f-cad565b447d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{d}a+10\\,\\text{d}b+2\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338458} {:answer/id #uuid "c538e2cd-8d1b-5596-aa21-b9ed6fa6490b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{d}a+5\\,\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338459} {:answer/id #uuid "65b26759-b0a8-503d-9815-8be3f6b0f7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{d}a+25\\,\\text{d}b+4\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed600a-5be6-506b-b39e-adaa571f4ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338457} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d50eb7a-d236-5781-928f-7c8bc422f0e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "480ed0ae-75be-53e3-a415-b9cdb724ffd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-79746" :question/problem "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂b})\\,\\text{d}b+(\\frac{∂V}{∂c})\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =bc\\,\\text{d}a+ac\\,\\text{d}b+ab\\,\\text{d}c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,5,1) & =5\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}a+2\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}b+5\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8a7e49-44a5-5eb2-aa5f-e188de230361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338463} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ddff9-84da-5e06-acd2-bd26bff884c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338464} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec500fb-9d6b-535f-885b-c3e6cea4a712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338465} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f1a93-50dc-520d-b3f6-034e4c8f70a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338466} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ad2ee9-88ff-5e3c-bf97-74242a23b602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a7e49-44a5-5eb2-aa5f-e188de230361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338463} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ff3702-d8e8-5826-912c-9b84a056c099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c7b68c-e2a2-583a-bdbf-0d748565e794" :question/math-academy-id "q-79810" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+\\sqrt{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc980bd8-76e4-56aa-b098-dda0efd7cf1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338470} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e5382f-cb04-50cd-9a05-357a6a71400b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338471} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cf4b78-1acf-5e7e-acc0-0a4c1a06fad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338472} {:answer/id #uuid "e61cf5ba-8012-5ed1-927e-cbfb7e293016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338473} {:answer/id #uuid "6e069f8d-fcb8-5999-9c7a-542c943da00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e61cf5ba-8012-5ed1-927e-cbfb7e293016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338473} :answer-field/id #uuid "4195aeda-3179-5193-a7c1-1a1a96af71bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15304540-cd8c-5704-ac94-52aa9f0b8729" :question/math-academy-id "q-79813" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=xy+x{e}^{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y+{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}x+(x+x{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,0) & =(0+{e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}x+(2+2\\cdot {e}^{0})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+4\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(1,-0.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-0.5-0=-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+4\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-0.5) \\\\  & ≈-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec23fb49-327d-57eb-89f6-6928cecdd1c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338477} {:answer/id #uuid "1cea4b98-3707-5124-bdb8-96ae4bfb9f37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338478} {:answer/id #uuid "51eb6df5-a9c5-5682-85e2-ff6354f038f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338479} {:answer/id #uuid "98679f0f-81fb-568d-a7e3-812f76f7c74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338480} {:answer/id #uuid "835f9062-a830-5600-9799-531779ef052f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98679f0f-81fb-568d-a7e3-812f76f7c74e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338480} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa644c6e-aeab-5c7b-84d4-59838b44f193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae0f6dae-8b10-584d-b0c4-229329da28f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79815" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92f0f398-77ee-5a9c-bf8f-6108428c5c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123881} {:answer/id #uuid "db857478-b9e7-5904-936e-2df9f914ddb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123882} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e6ec59-1a4b-5ba9-b2e4-4b4890173871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123883} {:answer/id #uuid "125f914b-3c96-55bb-9e87-5ce0a478f12f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123884} {:answer/id #uuid "1e30e9ee-f957-5948-a933-88923312b6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e30e9ee-f957-5948-a933-88923312b6c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123885} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d7bbc04-93c9-52bc-976c-534299470382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d809b121-2117-5794-bb89-efcea5057754" :question/math-academy-id "q-79837" :question/problem "Estimate $(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91)=f(1.81,0.91),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.81,0.91).$\n\nMoving from $(2,1)$ to $(1.81,0.91),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.81-2=-0.19, \\\\ Δy & =0.91-1=-0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (-0.19)+2\\cdot (-0.09) \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.18.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.81,0.91) & ≈f(2,1)-0.18 \\\\ (1.81)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.91) & ≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-0.18 \\\\  & ≈0-0.18 \\\\  & ≈-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "acafc931-a294-5ca7-8e39-cf8be29f08a9" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e57e6c52-c828-5c7c-b563-65377c9f2d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338484} {:answer/id #uuid "e218bd7e-ff26-5214-8942-ab669e441e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.915" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338485} {:answer/id #uuid "daa6e67f-0752-5b9e-aa6c-be0c42951aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338486} {:answer/id #uuid "a1eba0d9-7e20-5ca0-8446-59e8494f2862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.930" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338487} {:answer/id #uuid "40365a43-4300-50a3-ab28-5794d774974e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.910" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daa6e67f-0752-5b9e-aa6c-be0c42951aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338486} :answer-field/id #uuid "c608a199-01ac-52ae-ba30-2f6c53784491" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7a466ad-861f-5b55-a550-74aad2ec19b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79840" :question/problem "Estimate $\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ at the point $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(4,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(4+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δx+1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9)=f(4.1,0.9),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(4.1,0.9).$\n\nMoving from $(4,1)$ to $(4.1,0.9),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =4.1-4=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.9-1=-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (0.1)+1(-0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.025-0.1 \\\\  & ≈-0.075.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.075.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4.1,0.9) & ≈f(4,1)-0.075 \\\\ \\sqrt{4.1}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.9) & ≈\\sqrt{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-0.075 \\\\  & ≈2-0.075 \\\\  & ≈1.925.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f23c5c7-d281-54da-b625-412925c80667" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Differential of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e9b298e-62a1-5c70-b7b0-749d6774c23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,\\text{d}x+2y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338491} {:answer/id #uuid "7d94c2db-c35f-5137-bd5a-3cbf10391722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338492} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c2e517-da84-5d42-a4e2-8d83d8b5f872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338493} {:answer/id #uuid "23b5ed90-b670-5b2f-a3b1-f45611051696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338494} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7bd9d2-4130-5cdd-91f9-988ecfc04e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e9b298e-62a1-5c70-b7b0-749d6774c23f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,\\text{d}x+2y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338491} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b261f7-ba96-5f4f-98e2-c920d659c8d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4cf2d9e-3d03-57db-bef6-4b2ef0739dbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79955" :question/problem "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+2y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "153739f2-df62-5baf-8438-225edfc33ecb" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Differential in a Geometrical Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa44f3c6-8769-51eb-805e-e8f137d31288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338498} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f626ac-ae43-552c-8347-f73795ee47b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338499} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ac7499-57a6-5bc2-9378-221f1148e78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338500} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6abe86-fa8f-581b-a002-e257642411a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{d}r+4\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338501} {:answer/id #uuid "2faef51e-33d2-5208-8d1d-de99b9d98016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{3}{10}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4ac7499-57a6-5bc2-9378-221f1148e78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3551fa-725a-54f5-856a-ec21465a741b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74293020-87b8-5b53-8917-b5f49fa422c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-79958" :question/problem "Given a right cone, find the differential $\\text{d}l$ for the slant height $l$ of the right cone if its base radius is $r=3$ and height $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The slant height of the right cone is $l=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}l(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂l}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂l}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=3$  and $h=4$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(3,4) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
